## What I did

Commented on #835 (shared brace scanner), kept open. This session's Task 1 was direct evidence for that issue's thesis, so it was worth reporting even though it fixed nothing there:

src/safety.rs held four independent enumerations of "what counts as a shell operator." Three listed four operators; one listed two. That two-element one had already shipped a real miss — git status && > important.txt was flagged as a bare truncation, git status || > important.txt was not. I folded three sites into one COMMAND_SEPARATORS const.

I included the half that argues against a blanket dedup: I refused to fold the fourth site (check_rm_destruction carries a bare & and answers a narrower question) and refused to fold `detect_write_command's wider char class (folding (/)/backtick would read echo $(foo) > bar` as a bare truncation, which it isn't). So the transferable rule is narrower than "deduplicate the small unit" — it's decide whether the two copies answer the same question, and write the answer at the const either way. Verified before writing: one const, three consumers, 101 safety tests green, fourth site still separate at :384.

